\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZhy{}\PYGZhy{} We represent a \PYGZdq{}subset\PYGZdq{} as a predicate α → Prop.}
\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZhy{}\PYGZhy{} This is the same definition as `Set α` from Chapter 1}
\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZhy{}\PYGZhy{} (`def Set (α : Type) : Type := α → Prop`).}
\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZhy{}\PYGZhy{} We use `abbrev` rather than `def` so that Lean treats `Pred α`}
\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZhy{}\PYGZhy{} as completely transparent — unfolding it automatically during}
\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZhy{}\PYGZhy{} elaboration without needing `simp` or `unfold`.  This makes the}
\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZhy{}\PYGZhy{} `sSup`/`sInf` signatures below easier to work with.}
\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZhy{}\PYGZhy{} `def Set` was opaque because we wanted `Set α` to have its own}
\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZhy{}\PYGZhy{} identity for attaching instances; here we just want a readable alias.}
